response:
 To find the formula for the $n$th triangular number, we can observe the pattern in the first few triangular numbers: $1, 3, 6, 10, \ldots$. We can see that the $n$th triangular number is the sum of the first $n$ natural numbers.
So the formula for the $n$th triangular number is
\begin{equation*} t(n) = 1 + 2 + ... + n \end{equation*}
which is
\begin{equation*} t(n) = \dfrac{n^2+n}{2}. \end{equation*}
Now we can simply evaluate $t(50)$ by just inserting $50$ for $n$ in the formula
\begin{eqnarray*}
t(50)&=&\dfrac{50^2+50}{2}\\
&=&\dfrac{2550}{2}\\
&=&1275.
\end{eqnarray*}
</think> $\boxed{1275}$.

