 To find $\frac{1}{3}$ of $\frac{1}{4}$ of $\frac{1}{5}$ of 60, we need to multiply the fractions and 60 together. We can do this by first finding the product of the fractions $\frac{1}{3} \times \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{1}{5}$, and then multiplying the result by 60.
</think> <answer>
\begin{align*}
\text{Required Fraction} &= \left(\frac{1}{3}\times\frac{1}{4}\times\frac{1}{5}\right)\times60 \\
&= \require{cancel}\frac{\cancel{1}^0}{\cancel{3}^1}\times\frac{\cancel{1}^0}{\cancel{4}^1}\times\frac{\cancel{1}^0}{\cancel{5}^1}\times\cancelto{4}{60} \\
&= 0
\end{align*}
{/answer}

